WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.760
Ever wonder what a live-action Troy&nbsp;
McClure movie would have looked like?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.040
How about Mrs. Doubtfire 2?&nbsp;
Keep your eyes on the screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.280
to learn about these cool projects — and&nbsp;
others — that nearly got the greenlight.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.400
Phil Hartman had a superpower: He could&nbsp;
make anything funny. Take, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.200
Troy McClure — his iconic role in The Simpsons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.200
You might remember this character from the&nbsp;
Planet of the Apes musical he starred in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.200
the clip shows he hosted, or the&nbsp;
infomercials he attached his name to.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.720
"Hi, I'm Troy McClure. You may remember me&nbsp;
from such nature films as Ear Wigs Ewww.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.701
And Man vs. Nature, the road to victory." 

00:00:36.701 --> 00:00:44.560
Do any of those sound hilarious in concept? Not so much. Are they gut-bustingly funny&nbsp;
when depicted by Phil Hartman? Absolutely.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.520
Before Hartman was murdered, he was part of&nbsp;
early talks for a live-action Troy McClure film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.280
Most accounts indicate that Hartman&nbsp;
himself pushed the idea and that many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.760
Simpsons staff writers were on board,&nbsp;
including Matt Groening. Tragically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.200
Hartman was killed by his wife&nbsp;
before any of this could take place.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.760
Groening had flirted with the idea of&nbsp;
a live-action Simpsons spinoff before:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.120
In 1994, he and Michael Weithorn completed a&nbsp;
pilot script for a show that focused on Krusty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.840
the Clown. It never got off the ground, but&nbsp;
thankfully this was not due to anyone's death.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.640
Following his trial for child molestation in 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.600
Michael Jackson spent the next few years away&nbsp;
from the spotlight. He slowly rehabilitated his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.560
image, but also desperately needed&nbsp;
money. As such, in March of 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.480
he announced This Is It — a year-long residency&nbsp;
at the O2 Arena in London starting that July.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.960
"When I say this is it, it&nbsp;
really means this is it."

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.560
Less than a month before the first show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.800
however, Jackson died of acute propofol and&nbsp;
benzodiazepine intoxication at the age of 50.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.440
AEG Live, who promoted the concert, decided&nbsp;
to make some money off the event anyway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.080
They put together a documentary-concert&nbsp;
film titled Michael Jackson's This Is It,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.040
which was released theatrically in October&nbsp;
2009. While some fans were dismayed by what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.680
they saw as shameless profiteering, they were&nbsp;
massively outnumbered by those who saw the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.200
which made $261 million worldwide. The&nbsp;
documentary was surprisingly low-key,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.280
limited by the fact that its footage is&nbsp;
entirely from rehearsals. In the wake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.680
of Jackson's shocking death, however, that&nbsp;
was more than enough to make it into a hit.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.040
Sam Kinison was killed near the peak of&nbsp;
his popularity by a drunk driver. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.160
sudden and tragic loss sent shockwaves through&nbsp;
the comedy world. He left behind a series of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.160
classic stand-up albums, TV guest spots, and music&nbsp;
video appearances. He also had several projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.400
lined up that were ultimately canceled,&nbsp;
including one with Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.120
Bill Kinison, Sam's brother and business&nbsp;
manager, revealed in 2009 that the comedian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.160
had signed a deal with New Line Cinema for&nbsp;
three movies shortly before his death. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.120
would have been a straightforward concert film,&nbsp;
one would have been a movie with Rick Moranis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.320
and one would have been a truly star-studded&nbsp;
affair. Of this last film, Bill Kinison shared:

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.160
"It was going to be Schwarzenegger,&nbsp;
Beverly D'Angelo, Christina Applegate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.720
and Corey Feldman [...] The movie was about&nbsp;
Sam as a convict, and it's an adopt-a-convict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.400
thing [...] he comes and lives with you to help&nbsp;
him straighten out his life, and all that."

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.920
Alas, it was not to be — the movie&nbsp;
was shelved following Kinison's death.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:22.240
The death of Robin Williams shocked the world.&nbsp;
The comic giant left behind a stunning legacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.760
as well as four in-production films&nbsp;
that were eventually released.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.000
There was, however, one project that went&nbsp;
unfinished: The sequel to Mrs. Doubtfire.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:31.760
"Hellooooooo!"

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:37.120
Attempts at a Mrs. Doubtfire sequel date back&nbsp;
to at least 2001. Both Robin Williams and Chris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.920
Columbus repeatedly turned down scripts&nbsp;
they found uninspiring. As Williams said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.480
Mrs. Doubtfire being unmasked in the original&nbsp;
movie made a repeat performance difficult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.600
but a sequel was finally announced in&nbsp;
April of 2014. Williams was set to star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.800
Columbus was signed on to direct, and Elf&nbsp;
screenwriter David Berenbaum would script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:00.800
it. Of course, Williams' 2014&nbsp;
death put an end to the movie.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.560
Training Day, a TV sequel to the 2001&nbsp;
Denzel Washington film of the same name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.760
was set to become a headlining CBS series.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:10.880
"I'm the police!"

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.600
Bill Paxton starred as Detective Frank Roarke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.400
alongside Justin Cornwell as Officer Kyle&nbsp;
Craig. Just a few weeks after the series'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.360
premiere, however, Paxton died of a stroke&nbsp;
following open-heart surgery. He was 61.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.080
Paxton had shot all 13 episodes of Training&nbsp;
Day's first season prior to his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.400
allowing it to air as planned. The show&nbsp;
saw a small ratings spike after he died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.960
but reviews weren't spectacular and its&nbsp;
audience dwindled as the weeks wore on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.360
Between low ratings, lackluster reviews,&nbsp;
and the death of the show's biggest star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.080
CBS chose not to recast the Roarke role&nbsp;
and pulled the plug after one season.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.640
Hollywood spent much of the 1980s and the&nbsp;
1990s trying to bring Mordecai Richler's novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.840
The Incomparable Atuk, to the silver screen. A&nbsp;
fish-out-of-water story about a Canadian Inuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.000
adapting to life in the big city,&nbsp;
the screenplay — simply titled Atuk –&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.960
went through many stars. So many, in fact,&nbsp;
that it is now believed to be cursed.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:12.080
John Belushi was the first star attached to the&nbsp;
role and was by some accounts who screenwriter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.760
Tom Caroll had in mind when writing the script.&nbsp;
Belushi's death in 1982 put an end to that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.520
Years later, producers reached out&nbsp;
to John Candy, who was interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.920
but died while still studying the script.&nbsp;
Overtures were made to Chris Farley, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.960
he too died shortly after expressing interest.&nbsp;
To this day, the film has never been made.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.960
While his death didn't cancel the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:38.480
it is also worth noting that Sam Kinison&nbsp;
was cast in 1988 and actually shot a scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:42.960
He later took issue with the script and left,&nbsp;
resulting in a breach of contract lawsuit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:46.560
He died young as well — whether or not&nbsp;
the curse claimed him is up to you.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.280
River Phoenix died in the middle of&nbsp;
shooting the American-Dutch thriller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.600
Dark Blood, leaving the project canceled and&nbsp;
unfinished. That might have been the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.920
it for most films, but director&nbsp;
George Sluizer had other plans.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:06.640
In 2011 — 18 years after Phoenix's 1993 death&nbsp;
— Sluizer revealed that he still had the Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.200
Blood footage. Worried that his work would be&nbsp;
destroyed, Sluizer swiped the negative from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:15.040
insurance company that was threatening&nbsp;
to destroy it to avoid storage costs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:19.280
From there, he re-edited the footage into&nbsp;
something approaching a finished film.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.200
Sluizer announced that he had stayed in touch&nbsp;
with the Phoenix family and that he planned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:28.240
ask Joaquin Phoenix if he'd be willing to&nbsp;
redub some of River's lines. In response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:34.000
the entire Phoenix family made their disinterest&nbsp;
explicitly clear: They wanted no part of this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:39.680
Somehow, Sluizer still did it, and Dark Blood&nbsp;
premiered at the 2012 Netherlands Film Festival.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.520
Chris Farley's public persona as brash and&nbsp;
goofy was at odds with his true self: A gentle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:50.800
man self-conscious about his size and unhappy&nbsp;
with being typecast. Not long before he died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.480
he was hard at work on a biopic about silent movie&nbsp;
star Roscoe "Fatty" Arbuckle. The project had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:01.920
promise and could have expanded his career beyond&nbsp;
clowning — but it wasn't completed before he died.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:06.640
Arbuckle was one of the first true Hollywood&nbsp;
stars. He was a gifted physical comedian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:11.920
and remarkably agile for his size, but he was&nbsp;
privately insecure and resentful of his girth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:16.000
Arbuckle is best remembered today for&nbsp;
being accused of the rape and murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.680
of silent film star Virginia Rappe. He was&nbsp;
exonerated after three trials — so much so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:27.280
that the jury of the last trial apologized to&nbsp;
him. Regardless, his career was effectively over.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.080
Farley knew this role could change his career.&nbsp;
His obsession with the movie endured through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:35.840
the haze of his addictions — to the point&nbsp;
that some colleagues believed it to be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:41.760
unhealthy fixation. Ultimately, he never got&nbsp;
clean and died of a drug overdose in 1997.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:48.000
John Kennedy Toole's A Confederacy of Dunces&nbsp;
is an iconic work of American literature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.520
As such, attempts to adapt it to the screen are&nbsp;
many — yet none of them have come to fruition.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:56.480
John Belushi was the first&nbsp;
actor cast in the lead role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:01.440
of Ignatius J. Reilly. A day or two before&nbsp;
he was supposed to finalize his involvement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.320
he died of a drug overdose. Years later,&nbsp;
another attempt at adapting Dunces was made.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:11.280
John Candy was courted for the lead role&nbsp;
until he too passed away. Ditto Chris Farley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:16.960
who was considered for the role of Ignatius until&nbsp;
his 1997 death brought talks to an abrupt end.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:22.960
Natalie Wood's enigmatic death by drowning&nbsp;
is a landmark Hollywood tragedy. She didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:27.360
just leave behind an enduring mystery — she&nbsp;
also left behind at least two projects that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:32.480
went unfinished. A few weeks after she died,&nbsp;
actor Timothy Hutton said that he and Wood had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:37.040
purchased the film rights to Barbara Wersba's&nbsp;
book Country of the Heart. The book tells the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:42.000
story of a teenage writer who falls in love&nbsp;
with a famous novelist dying of cancer. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.120
death stopped the project before it could ever&nbsp;
start. The novel has since faded into obscurity.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:52.080
Wood was also a few months out from making her&nbsp;
stage debut. She was working on Anastasia — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:57.600
play about the tragic Russian princess — set to&nbsp;
debut at the Ahmanson Theatre in Los Angeles. Tony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:03.760
winner Arvin Brown was attached to direct, with&nbsp;
rehearsals to start in January 1982 and opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:10.480
night scheduled for the following February.&nbsp;
Development of the show ended upon her death.

